# Ownership is the last line of defence when a change is textually innocuous but
# semantically a policy change. Moving a tool version in tools.lock, widening a
# role's parameter contract, or editing an install script all look like routine
# edits and are all trust decisions.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS FILE AUTO-ASSIGNS REVIEWERS. IT IS NOT A MERGE REQUIREMENT.
#
# `infrashift` has one member. Enabling "Require review from Code Owners" while
# that is true would block every pull request: GitHub does not let you approve
# your own PR, so the only way to merge would be an admin bypass on every
# change -- which trains the habit of bypassing the gate and leaves a repo whose
# protection is nominal.
#
# So the paths below exist to surface *what kind of change this is* on the PR,
# and to be correct the day the org grows. They do not gate anything today.
#
# When a second maintainer joins: create the teams, swap the owners below, and
# turn on "Require review from Code Owners" in the same change -- see
# SETUP-ENVIRONMENTS.md. Until then, do not enable it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

*                                   @ryancraig

# --- Trust decisions -------------------------------------------------------
/.github/pdp/                       @ryancraig
/.github/pdp/policies.rego          @ryancraig
/.github/pdp/policies_test.rego     @ryancraig
/.github/pdp/public-keys/           @ryancraig

# These binaries run in the job that holds the signing key, so a version bump
# here is a supply-chain decision.
/tools.lock                         @ryancraig
/scripts/install-tools.sh           @ryancraig

# --- Pipeline engines ------------------------------------------------------
/.github/workflows/                 @ryancraig
/scripts/                           @ryancraig

# --- What ships inside a feature -------------------------------------------
# install.sh and the Ansible role are what actually execute in a developer's
# container. bootstrap is the runner every other feature depends on, so a change
# there affects all of them at once.
/src/                               @ryancraig
/src/bootstrap/                     @ryancraig

# --- Repository security posture -------------------------------------------
/.github/CODEOWNERS                 @ryancraig
/.gitleaks.toml                     @ryancraig
/.gitignore                         @ryancraig
/SETUP-ENVIRONMENTS.md              @ryancraig

# --- Documentation ---------------------------------------------------------
/docs/                              @ryancraig
/README.md                          @ryancraig
